[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Rigidbody, Colliders, and Physics Layers in Unity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Physics simulation is the backbone of realistic gameplay in Unity. Understanding how Rigidbodies, Colliders, and Physics Layers interact is essential for building performant and responsive game mechanics."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The Rigidbody Component: Simulating Mass and Gravity"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Rigidbody component places an object under the control of Unity's physics engine. By adding a Rigidbody, your GameObject gains mass, drag, and responds to forces and gravity."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mass: Determines the weight of the object, affecting collision outcomes with other rigidbodies."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Is Kinematic: When checked, the Rigidbody is moved via code (Transform position) rather than physics forces, ignoring gravity and collisions while still affecting other non-kinematic rigidbodies."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Colliders: Defining Physical Boundaries"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"While Rigidbodies define how an object moves, Colliders define its physical shape. Unity provides various collider types, such as BoxCollider, SphereCollider, CapsuleCollider, and MeshCollider."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Primitive Colliders are lightweight and computationally cheap. Always prefer primitives or composite primitive shapes over complex Mesh Colliders."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Is Trigger: Converts the collider into a trigger zone. Triggers do not block physical movement; instead, they fire events like OnTriggerEnter when another object enters their volume."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Physics Layers and Collision Matrix"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Physics Layers allow you to group GameObjects and define which layers can collide with one another. This is crucial for optimizing performance and preventing unwanted physical interactions, such as bullets ignoring the player who fired them."},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can configure the Collision Matrix by navigating to Edit > Project Settings > Physics."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Practical Implementation: Applying Force via Code"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"To interact with a Rigidbody programmatically, you should obtain a reference to the component in your C# script and apply forces within FixedUpdate."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"using UnityEngine;

public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    private Rigidbody rb;
    public float speed = 5f;

    void Start()
    {
        rb = GetComponent<Rigidbody>();
    }

    void FixedUpdate()
    {
        float moveHorizontal = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");
        float moveVertical = Input.GetAxis("Vertical");

        Vector3 movement = new Vector3(moveHorizontal, 0.0f, moveVertical);
        rb.AddForce(movement * speed);
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always apply physics-based movements and force calculations inside FixedUpdate instead of Update to ensure deterministic behavior."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid scaling objects with Mesh Colliders dynamically, as rebuilding a convex mesh collider is computationally expensive."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Physics Layers wisely to minimize unnecessary collision checks between static environment objects."}]
